      "title": "SMB2: Remove Fixed Part/Dynamic Part subfields of StructureSize",
      "message": "SMB2: Remove Fixed Part/Dynamic Part subfields of StructureSize\n\nThe StructureSize field in a SMB2 message is fixed for the type\nof message (usually determined just from the opcode and whether it\nis a request or response). Many messages are variable length. While\nin general the StructureSize used is calculated as though the variable\nsize were 1 byte, there are several exceptions. A NEGOTIATE Request\nhas a variable length portion, but the StructureSize indicates only\nthe fixed length. A WRITE Response has a fixed size of 16, but the\nStructureSize is always 17 even though there is no variable portion.\nA LOCK Request has a variable length, but StructureSize is calculated\nto indicate the structure with one SMB2_LOCK_ELEMENT child structure,\nmaking the least significant bit (previously interpreted as a flag\nindicating a variable length portion) zero.\n\nSince the rule (possibly derived from reverse engineering before\nMicrosoft was so forthcoming with specification documents) does not\nhold, abandon the rule and just display the StructureSize (with a\nexplanatory blurb.)\n\nFix #16078\n\nAI-Assisted: no\n",
      "author_name": "John Thacker",
